\documentclass[11pt]{article}

\usepackage[review]{acl}

\usepackage{times}
\usepackage{latexsym}
\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}
\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}
\usepackage{microtype}
\usepackage{graphicx}
\usepackage{booktabs}
\usepackage{amsmath}
\usepackage{amssymb}
\usepackage{multirow}
\usepackage{xcolor}

\title{Per-Example Predictive Multiplicity in Hybrid Recurrent Architectures}

\author{Anonymous}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Hybrid recurrent architectures that interleave attention layers with linear recurrence are increasingly standard, yet how attention affects per-example behavior across different recurrent backbones remains poorly quantified. We conduct a systematic analysis using the controlled model zoo of \citet{wang2025hybrid}: three architectures (GatedDeltaNet, RetNet, HGRN2) at five attention ratios and two scales (340M and 1.3B parameters), evaluated on 10 benchmarks with per-example analysis on four (LAMBADA, BoolQ, HellaSwag, ARC-Easy; $N = 5{,}153$ to $10{,}042$). Aggregate convergence is task-specific: only LAMBADA shows monotonic reduction in cross-architecture variance ($\rho = -1.0$, BCa 95\% CI $[-1.0, -0.7]$; $\sigma$ drops 79\%; Bonferroni-adjusted $p = .100$), complete at 1.3B but non-monotonic at 340M. Per-example predictive multiplicity varies in magnitude but is present on all four benchmarks: the ambiguity $\alpha$ \citep{marx2020predictive} ranges from 0.10 (HellaSwag) to 0.49 (BoolQ), forming a task-dependent spectrum rather than a binary phenomenon, while pairwise Jaccard of helped/hurt sets remains universally low ($\leq 0.13$, all $p < .01$). On LAMBADA, aggregate and per-example trajectories are decoupled: $\sigma$ decreases gradually while per-example overlap saturates at 4\% attention. These results establish per-example predictive multiplicity across all four task categories (retrieval, comprehension, reasoning, mixed) in three linear recurrent backbones, with HellaSwag's near-zero $\alpha$ serving as a reference point that rules out measurement noise.
\end{abstract}

\input{sections/introduction}

\input{sections/related_work}

\input{sections/method}

\input{sections/experiments}

\input{sections/conclusion}

\section*{Limitations}

Our analysis has several limitations that constrain the scope of its conclusions.

\paragraph{Statistical power.} With only $n = 5$ attention ratios, the minimum achievable two-tailed $p$-value under an exact permutation test is approximately 0.008. This ceiling limits our ability to detect weak convergence effects and contributes to the Bonferroni-adjusted $p = .100$ for LAMBADA not reaching conventional significance. We report confidence intervals as a complementary characterization to partially address this constraint.

\paragraph{Per-example scope.} Our per-example analysis covers four benchmarks (LAMBADA, BoolQ, HellaSwag, ARC-Easy) spanning all four task categories. Extending to additional benchmarks within each category and to other hybrid families would further characterize the multiplicity spectrum.

\paragraph{Scale coverage.} We compare two scale points (340M and 1.3B parameters). The contrast between complete convergence at 1.3B and partial convergence at 340M is a binary observation, not a scaling law. Intermediate and larger scales would be needed to characterize the functional relationship between model capacity and convergence.

\paragraph{Architecture coverage.} Our study examines three recurrent architectures (GatedDeltaNet, RetNet, HGRN2) from one model zoo. Other hybrid families, including Jamba, RWKV-6, and Hymba, use different mixing strategies that may produce different per-example patterns. Generalization beyond these three architectures requires additional controlled experiments. Each configuration in the model zoo corresponds to a single training run; we cannot disentangle architectural effects from seed-specific effects.

\paragraph{Observational design.} Our attention weight analysis is descriptive, not causal. We report cosine similarities and entropy differences between helped and hurt examples but cannot determine whether attention patterns cause the per-example effects or merely correlate with them. Interventional methods such as attention head ablation would be necessary to establish causal links.

\paragraph{Pre-training only.} All models in our study are evaluated after pre-training without fine-tuning. Per-example effects may change under task-specific adaptation, and the decoupling pattern we observe may not persist after fine-tuning.

\section*{Ethics Statement}

This work analyzes publicly available pretrained language models from the model zoo of \citet{wang2025hybrid}. No new data collection, human subjects research, or model training is involved. We identify per-example behavioral differences across architectures, which may inform practitioners about the limitations of aggregate benchmark comparisons but does not introduce new risks.

\input{arr_checklist}

\bibliography{references}

\appendix
\input{sections/appendix}

\end{document}

\section{Experiments}\label{sec:experiments}

We analyze how attention affects cross-architecture performance variance using models from the hybrid recurrent model zoo of \citet{wang2025hybrid}. Our analysis spans three architectures (GatedDeltaNet, RetNet, HGRN2), five attention ratios (0\% pure recurrent, 4\%, 7.7\%, 14.3\%, 25\%), and two scales (340M and 1.3B parameters). In the h$k$-1 notation used throughout, one attention layer is interleaved per every $k{+}1$ layers; h3-1 denotes 25\% attention. All models within each scale share identical training data and hyperparameters; they differ only in architecture and attention configuration. We evaluate on 10 benchmarks spanning retrieval, comprehension, reasoning, and mixed task categories, plus one supplementary benchmark (COPA, $n = 100$).

\subsection{Task-Specific Convergence}\label{ssec:task_convergence}

We first ask whether adding attention layers reduces performance differences between architectures, and whether any such reduction is uniform across tasks. For each benchmark, we compute the cross-architecture standard deviation ($\sigma$) at each attention ratio and measure the Spearman rank correlation ($\rho$) between attention ratio and $\sigma$ across the five configurations.

Table~\ref{tab:convergence} reports results for all benchmarks at 1.3B scale. LAMBADA is the only benchmark exhibiting monotonic convergence: $\rho = -1.0$ (exact permutation test, $p = .017$, two-tailed, $n = 5$, 120 permutations). Cross-architecture $\sigma$ drops from 0.024 to 0.005, a 79\% reduction. The 95\% BCa bootstrap confidence interval for $\rho$ is $[-1.0, -0.7]$ (10{,}000 resamples), which excludes zero. After Bonferroni correction for six confirmatory benchmarks, the adjusted $p = .100$ does not reach conventional significance at $\alpha = 0.05$; the confidence interval provides a complementary characterization indicating a reliably negative association.

LAMBADA is a pure context-retrieval task \citep{paperno2016lambada}; attention provides direct token-level access to the full context, consistent with convergence reflecting compensation for differences in how recurrent architectures store contextual information. No other benchmark shows significant convergence (all $|\rho| \leq 0.7$, $p \geq .233$). ARC-Easy and MMLU exhibit moderate negative correlations ($\rho = -0.7$) but non-monotonic trajectories; COPA shows a similar pattern ($\rho = -0.67$) but is limited by $n = 100$ (Figure~\ref{fig:task_specificity}). The decoupling analysis (\S\ref{ssec:decoupling}) is demonstrated on LAMBADA; per-example divergence extends to all four benchmarks with per-example analysis (\S\ref{ssec:per_example}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig2_task_specificity.pdf}
\caption{Spearman $\rho$ between attention ratio and cross-architecture $\sigma$ for each benchmark at 1.3B scale. LAMBADA ($\rho = -1.0$; BCa 95\% CI $[-1.0, -0.7]$) is the only benchmark with a negative correlation whose confidence interval excludes zero. Benchmarks are grouped by task category.}
\label{fig:task_specificity}
\end{figure}

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\caption{Cross-architecture convergence across benchmarks (1.3B scale). $\rho$: Spearman correlation between attention ratio and cross-architecture $\sigma$ across five configurations. $p$: exact permutation test (two-tailed, 120 permutations). $\sigma$ values reported $\times 10^{2}$ (accuracy percentage points). Ratio: $\sigma_{\text{pure}} / \sigma_{\text{h3-1}}$. \textbf{Bold}: only benchmark with $\rho = -1.0$.}
\label{tab:convergence}
\begin{tabular}{llrrrrr}
\toprule
